WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:32.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. Over the years in this job, I've learned a lot of cocktail party facts about the built environment. For example, the tallest building in the world is the Birch Khalifa in Dubai. But the largest building in the world by volume is a Boeing airplane factory in Everett, Washington. It's 472 million cubic feet. The longest bridge is in China, as is the longest wall. No surprise there. But what about the longest fence?
这里是《99%隐形》。我是罗曼·马尔斯。多年来，在这份工作中，我学到了很多关于建筑环境的酒会谈资。例如，世界上最高的建筑是迪拜的哈利法塔。但按体积计算，世界上最大的建筑是位于华盛顿州埃弗里特的波音飞机工厂。它有4.72亿立方英尺。最长的桥在中国，最长的墙也在中国。这并不令人惊讶。但最长的围栏呢？

2
00:00:32.000 --> 00:01:16.000
reporter Shirley Wong traveled deep into rural Australia to find it. The longest fence in the world begins just a few hours north of Brisbane on the outskirts of a small town called Gindawi. Jindawi has a hardware store, an accountant's office, a butcher shop, a beauty salon, and a small hotel on top of a pub. Oh, I've got a meat tray raffle. I love those. But I wasn't here to win a meat tray. I came to Jindawi to meet Doug Henning. He's a former tourism officer who agreed to give me a tour of the fence his town is famous for. Hi, so nice to meet you.
记者雪莉·黄深入澳大利亚农村去寻找它。世界上最长的围栏始于布里斯班以北仅几小时路程的一个叫金达威的小镇郊外。金达威有一家五金店、一家会计师事务所、一家肉店、一家美容院，还有一家位于酒吧楼上的小旅馆。哦，我参加了肉类拼盘抽奖。我喜欢那些。但我来这里不是为了赢取肉类拼盘。我来金达威是为了见道格·亨宁。他是一位前旅游官员，他同意带我参观他所在小镇闻名的围栏。嗨，很高兴见到你。

3
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Yeah, thank you. I've been watching the weather. Amazing. The clouds are going that way. So we should be lucky this afternoon. Okay, great. I met up with Doug outside the Jindawi Cultural Center right in the middle of town. And he was standing next to an almost seven foot tall sculpture of a dog.
是的，谢谢。我一直在观察天气。太棒了。云往那边飘。所以我们今天下午应该会很幸运。好的，太棒了。我在镇中心金达威文化中心外见到了道格。他正站在一座近七英尺高的狗雕塑旁边。

4
00:01:35.000 --> 00:02:11.000
It's all made from small pieces of metal, welded together. This metallic hound is a dingo, an animal that can only be found on the island continent of Australia. Dingoes are Australia's apex predator. They are smaller than wolves but can run just as fast. They are lean and extremely athletic. They have reddish blonde fur, white bellies, and pointy ears. They look kind of like, I don't know, buff, Shiba Inus. Given this giant prominent statue, you might think that the people of Gendawi worshipped the mighty dingo.
它完全由焊接在一起的小块金属制成。这只金属猎犬是一只澳洲野犬，一种只能在澳大利亚这个岛屿大陆上找到的动物。澳洲野犬是澳大利亚的顶级掠食者。它们比狼小，但跑得一样快。它们瘦削且极其强壮。它们有红金色的皮毛、白色的腹部和尖尖的耳朵。它们看起来有点像，我不知道，健壮的柴犬。鉴于这座巨大而显眼的雕像，你可能会认为金达威的人民崇拜强大的澳洲野犬。

5
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:50.000
But Doug informed me this is very much not the case. So there's not a lot of dingo fans in town? Not a great lot. In fact, Jindawi's famous fence was built to keep dingoes out. It's called the dingo barrier fence, and it stretches across the southeastern corner of the continent, separating dingoes from the part of the country where the vast majority of Australians actually live. And it is the longest continuous fence in the world. At over 5,000 kilometers, the dingo barrier fence is longer than the distance between San Francisco and New York City.
但道格告诉我，事实并非如此。所以镇上没有多少澳洲野犬的粉丝？不是很多。事实上，金达威著名的围栏是为了把澳洲野犬挡在外面而建的。它被称为澳洲野犬屏障围栏，横跨大陆的东南角，将澳洲野犬与绝大多数澳大利亚人实际居住的地区隔离开来。它是世界上最长的连续围栏。澳洲野犬屏障围栏全长超过5000公里，比旧金山到纽约市的距离还要长。

6
00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:30.000
And in a field just outside of Gendawi is where the fence starts. That big tree there is the true start of the fence and that goes that way. To be honest, it's not much to look at, just a regular wire fence that's about six feet tall to prevent dingoes from jumping over. I want to stand next to it and see if it goes above my head. I think it should. I'm 5'4. Okay, I'm like about the same height of it. You are standing on a 12-inch mound. I am standing on, okay, fair enough. The fence starts here in the middle of a cattle pasture, and from there it runs all the way across the con.
围栏的起点就在金达威外的一片田野里。那棵大树就是围栏真正的起点，然后往那边延伸。老实说，它看起来没什么特别的，只是一道普通的铁丝网围栏，大约六英尺高，用来防止澳洲野犬跳过去。我想站在它旁边看看它是否比我高。我想应该会。我身高5英尺4英寸。好的，我和它差不多高。你站在一个12英寸的土堆上。我站在，好吧，有道理。围栏从这里开始，位于一个牛牧场的中间，从那里它一直延伸穿过整个大陆。

7
00:03:30.000 --> 00:04:10.000
It just goes on and on and on all the way to South Australia. Yeah, yeah, that's lovely. The dingo barrier fence is a remarkable piece of infrastructure, and not just because of its length. This simple wire structure has actually transformed the entire ecology of Australia. It has split the continent in two, separating animal populations and changing the landscape so much that you can see the effects from space. When you drive around Australia, fences are everywhere. are everywhere. They're used to control the movement of a lot of different kinds of animals. In fact, the dingo fence was originally built to manage a different creature altogether.
它一直延伸，一直延伸，直到南澳大利亚。是的，是的，那太好了。澳洲野犬屏障围栏是一项了不起的基础设施，不仅仅是因为它的长度。这个简单的铁丝网结构实际上改变了澳大利亚的整个生态。它将大陆一分为二，分离了动物种群，并改变了景观，以至于你可以从太空中看到其影响。当你开车环游澳大利亚时，到处都是围栏。到处都是。它们被用来控制许多不同种类动物的活动。事实上，澳洲野犬围栏最初是为了管理另一种完全不同的生物而建造的。

8
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:50.000
When British colonizers came to Australia in the late 18th century, they wanted this new place to feel like home. And so they took the plants and animals of Europe with them. And a lot of these species were actually brought in, in a lot of cases, for hunting, to allow people to go hunt these animals that they're used to going to hunt in their home country. This is Thomas Newsom, an ecologist and associate professor at the University of Sydney. So foxes, for example, were brought in so people could hunt them. Cats were brought in, they were kept as pets and they established wild populations. But in Australia, we've got other species that were brought in, deer, pigs, goats.
当英国殖民者在18世纪末来到澳大利亚时，他们希望这个新地方感觉像家一样。于是他们带上了欧洲的动植物。在很多情况下，引进这些物种实际上是为了狩猎，让人们能够去猎杀他们在祖国习惯猎杀的动物。这是托马斯·纽瑟姆，悉尼大学的生态学家和副教授。所以，例如，引进狐狸是为了让人狩猎。猫被引进，它们被当作宠物饲养，并建立了野生种群。但在澳大利亚，我们还有其他引进的物种，鹿、猪、山羊。

9
00:04:50.000 --> 00:05:24.000
They were all brought in for some form of a purpose. But that didn't turn out so well. Because of Australia's island geography, the native plants and animals there evolved in relative isolation, which made them particularly vulnerable to invasive species. And without a large number of natural predators, many of these new European creatures ran riot. Unfortunately, all of those, ones I've listed and many more, have created devastating effects on our fauna. The most destructive invader was surprisingly the rabbit.
它们都是为了某种目的而被引进的。但结果并不好。由于澳大利亚的岛屿地理环境，那里的原生动植物在相对隔离的状态下进化，这使它们特别容易受到入侵物种的影响。而且由于缺乏大量的天然捕食者，许多这些新的欧洲生物横行无忌。不幸的是，我列出的所有这些以及更多物种，对我们的动物群造成了破坏性的影响。最具有破坏性的入侵者令人惊讶地是兔子。

10
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:57.000
Rabbits came to Australia all because of one man, Thomas Austin, whose brother shipped him 24 rabbits from England as a gift. Over the next three years, those 24 animals bred like, well, rabbits. And soon, there were thousands. The rabbits damaged crops, overgrazed grasses, spread diseases, dug holes, wherever they pleased. Basically, they were the worst gift ever and made one man go down in Australian history as the guy who caused the rabbit plague. People tried to hunt them and call them, but there were just too many.
兔子来到澳大利亚全是因为一个人，托马斯·奥斯汀，他的兄弟从英国寄给他24只兔子作为礼物。在接下来的三年里，那24只动物像，嗯，兔子一样繁殖。很快，就有成千上万只了。兔子破坏庄稼，过度放牧草地，传播疾病，随处挖洞。基本上，它们是有史以来最糟糕的礼物，并让一个人作为导致兔子瘟疫的人载入澳大利亚史册。人们试图猎杀和扑杀它们，但数量实在太多了。

11
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:48.000
And so in 1901, the government created a rabbit department and hired an inspector to study the rabbit population. He found that rabbits hadn't yet crossed into certain agricultural areas, and that gave him an idea. Through years and years of trying to trap these animals, which was unsuccessful, the next step is to try and create a barrier. Rabbits can't climb very easily. They hop, but not particularly high. And so the idea was that they would stop the rabbits with a fence. So rabbit-proof fences were established to try and stop and contain the spread of rabbits throughout Australia. They built three different fences in an effort to seal off a large portion of the continent as a rabbit-free zone. But the rabbits managed to find a way around all of them.
因此，在1901年，政府成立了一个兔子部门，并聘请了一名检查员来研究兔子种群。他发现兔子还没有进入某些农业区，这给了他一个主意。经过多年试图诱捕这些动物的尝试，但没有成功，下一步就是尝试建立一道屏障。兔子不容易爬上去。它们跳跃，但跳得不是特别高。所以想法是他们会用围栏挡住兔子。因此，建立了防兔围栏，试图阻止和控制兔子在澳大利亚的蔓延。他们建造了三道不同的围栏，试图将大陆的一大部分封锁为无兔区。但兔子设法绕过了所有这些围栏。

12
00:06:48.000 --> 00:07:22.000
It wasn't successful. Rabbits ended up going all throughout Australia. Other states built rabbit fences during this period that also failed. And so in the end, the government had all these rabbit fences across the country that were serving no purpose. Over the years, the fences began to disintegrate. But around this time, another European animal was taking over Australia. And soon, all those rusty rabbit fences would be given a new purpose. In the 1800s, wool was a critical material for the British textile industry.
它没有成功。兔子最终遍布了整个澳大利亚。其他州在此期间也建造了防兔围栏，也都失败了。所以最终，政府在全国各地都有这些毫无用处的防兔围栏。多年来，围栏开始解体。但就在这时，另一种欧洲动物正在接管澳大利亚。很快，所有那些生锈的防兔围栏将被赋予新的用途。在19世纪，羊毛是英国纺织工业的关键材料。

13
00:07:22.000 --> 00:08:06.000
But Great Britain didn't have enough land to produce the sheep themselves. So they turned to their colony down under. In 1802, Lord Hobart, who's one of the pooh bars of the British colonial administration, he says, you know, absolutely we want to encourage these colonists to develop fine wool that can suit our manufacturers. That's Trish Fitzsimmons, co-author of the book Fleeced, unraveling the history of wool and war. Trish says that for the first time ever, the reddish landscape of Australia began to be dotted with little white sheep. And over time, the little white dots multiplied.
但英国没有足够的土地来自己生产羊毛。所以他们转向了他们南方的殖民地。1802年，英国殖民政府的头目之一霍巴特勋爵说，你知道，我们绝对希望鼓励这些殖民者开发适合我们制造商的优质羊毛。这是特里希·菲茨西蒙斯，《羊毛与战争的历史》一书的合著者。特里希说，这是有史以来第一次，澳大利亚红色的土地上开始点缀着小白羊。随着时间的推移，小白点越来越多。

14
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:58.000
By 1880, there were 100 million sheep in Australia, nearly 50 times the number of people. Australian wool was particularly critical for the British military. Wool was the best material for the battlefield because it was waterproof and flame resistant. And by the time World War I rolled around, it was clear that the countries with access to wool had a huge strategic advantage. World War I has got so many more soldiers than any previous war. I want to say 60 or 70 million. Each of those men need something like 20 sheep to support them, keep them alive, keep them warm, just in one year. World War I cemented wool as the foundation of the Australian economy.
到1880年，澳大利亚有1亿只羊，几乎是人口的50倍。澳大利亚羊毛对英国军队尤为关键。羊毛是战场上最好的材料，因为它防水且阻燃。当第一次世界大战来临时，很明显，拥有羊毛资源的国家具有巨大的战略优势。第一次世界大战的士兵比以往任何战争都多。我想说是6000万或7000万。这些士兵中的每一个人都需要大约20只羊来支持他们，让他们活下去，让他们保持温暖，仅仅在一年内。第一次世界大战巩固了羊毛作为澳大利亚经济基础的地位。

15
00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:40.000
But as the industry continued to grow, farmers needed more land for grazing. And so they started to breed sheep that could survive in these super dry marginal regions of the continent. And it was there that the economic superstar of Australia, the sheep, met its great adversary. The dingo. As the wool industry gathers ahead of steam, the areas of the continent, where sheep are grown, starts to expand out into the continent. Those sheep are vulnerable to dingoes. For dingoes, the expansion of sheep into the interior was like a free buffet.
但随着工业的持续增长，农民需要更多的土地来放牧。于是他们开始培育能在大陆这些超级干燥的边缘地区生存的羊。就在那里，澳大利亚的经济巨星——羊，遇到了它伟大的对手。澳洲野犬。随着羊毛工业的发展，大陆上养羊的地区开始向大陆内部扩张。这些羊很容易受到澳洲野犬的攻击。对于澳洲野犬来说，羊群向内部的扩张就像一场免费的自助餐。

16
00:09:40.000 --> 00:10:06.000
But for farmers, the situation was a nightmare. Dingoes are highly effective pack hunters. They are pretty much Australia's version of the big, bad wolf. And farmers would wake up and find that dozens of their sheep had been killed or maimed in dingo attacks. Because of the size of the flocks and their remote locations, it wasn't a simple problem to fix. The whole wool industry itself is set up,
但对于农民来说，情况是一场噩梦。澳洲野犬是高效的群体猎手。它们几乎是澳大利亚版的大灰狼。农民们醒来时会发现，几十只羊在澳洲野犬的袭击中被杀死或致残。由于羊群的规模和它们偏远的地理位置，这不是一个容易解决的问题。整个羊毛工业本身就是这样建立的，

17
00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:28.000
so you can't have predators. It's not financially viable to be out there protecting the sheep. That's Dr. Justine Philip, an environmental historian. We need to be able to put out these flocks of sheep, which number, you know, at least 2,000. You're not talking about a small flock. You're talking 2,000 and upwards, you know, 20,000. 100,000 sheep.
所以你不能有掠食者。在外面保护羊群在经济上是不可行的。这是贾斯汀·菲利普博士，一位环境历史学家。我们需要能够把这些羊群放出去，数量至少有2000只。你说的不是小羊群。你说的是2000只以上，你知道，2万只。10万只羊。

18
00:10:28.000 --> 00:11:07.000
And they basically put them out to pasture and they don't supervise them. So you can't do that without being pretty confident that you've got rid of all the predators in the surrounding area. Farmers wanted to completely eliminate this threat. And because the wool industry was so politically powerful, they got their wish. Australia went to war with the dingo. First, they created incentive programs that encourage farmers to kill dingoes. Soon, there were professional dingo hunters and trappers who were paid bounties for every dingo they killed.
他们基本上把它们放到牧场上，并不监督它们。所以如果不确信你已经消除了周围地区的所有掠食者，你就不能这样做。农民们想彻底消除这种威胁。而且因为羊毛工业在政治上非常强大，他们如愿以偿了。澳大利亚向澳洲野犬宣战。首先，他们制定了激励计划，鼓励农民杀死澳洲野犬。很快，就有专业的澳洲野犬猎人和诱捕者，他们每杀死一只澳洲野犬都会获得赏金。

19
00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:44.000
And then, after World War II, Australia ramped up its assault. A newspaper headline from 1946 read, D-Day for dingoes, above an article describing a plan to drop over 300,000 poisonous dingo baits across the landscape. And so that really brought the whole system up a notch, because instead of having to do ground baiting and doing everything on foot, they actually took the air on these airplanes, which had come back from the war and rent-in-use, and they had lots of pilots that were available for work. And so they started doing enormous aerial baiting projects across Australia.
然后，在第二次世界大战后，澳大利亚加强了攻击。1946年的一条报纸头条写着，“澳洲野犬的D日”，文章描述了一项计划，要在景观中投放超过30万个有毒的澳洲野犬诱饵。所以这确实让整个系统更上一层楼，因为他们不需要进行地面诱饵投放和徒步做所有事情，他们实际上使用了飞机，这些飞机是从战争中回来的，正在使用中，而且他们有很多飞行员可以工作。于是他们开始在澳大利亚各地进行大规模的空中诱饵投放项目。

20
00:11:44.000 --> 00:12:27.000
But at the same time they were slaughtering these animals, the wool industry was developing a different strategy, a plan to keep dingoes and sheep permanently separate. They would take all of those defunct rabbit fences and use them for dingoes instead. And so the rabbit-proof fences were given a dingo renovation. They joined the old rabbit fences together and raised them up to a height that could keep dingoes out. Eventually, they had a big dingo fence, which was once actually 9,600 kilometers long, and it's now still huge, five and a half thousand kilometers long. That's around 3,400 miles, cutting across scrublands and desert.
但与此同时，当他们屠杀这些动物时，羊毛工业正在制定一种不同的策略，一个让澳洲野犬和羊永久分离的计划。他们会拿走所有那些废弃的防兔围栏，转而用于澳洲野犬。于是防兔围栏进行了澳洲野犬改造。他们把旧的防兔围栏连接起来，并把它们加高到可以挡住澳洲野犬的高度。最终，他们有了一个巨大的澳洲野犬围栏，它曾经实际上有9600公里长，现在仍然很大，有5500公里长。大约是3400英里，横跨灌木丛和沙漠。

21
00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:58.000
And though the fence didn't effectively keep out rabbits, it did succeed in its second purpose. Thomas says the fence effectively sealed off nearly a third of the continent as a dingo-free zone. See, we're hitting this point here now, and it goes that way, then, that way, then it goes all the way. Oh, there's some wallabies over there. I see them galloping around. That's the kangaroo. Kangaroo, oh. Yep. I'm back in Jindawi in the eastern state of Queensland,
虽然围栏并没有有效地挡住兔子，但它确实实现了它的第二个目的。托马斯说，围栏有效地将近三分之一的大陆封锁为无澳洲野犬区。看，我们现在到了这个点，它往那边走，然后往那边，然后它一直走。哦，那边有一些小袋鼠。我看到它们在奔跑。那是袋鼠。袋鼠，哦。是的。我又回到了东部昆士兰州的金达威，

22
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:22.000
and I'm walking along the dingo fence with Doug Henning. Australia still spends big money on this fence, $10 million a year funded through state and local governments and a fence tax on sheep and cattle farmers. The fence is maintained by a team of patrolmen who divvy up the length and each take care of a section. Doug pointed to a hole that had been patched over. See this piece of mesh here?
我正和道格·亨宁沿着澳洲野犬围栏走。澳大利亚仍然在这道围栏上花费巨资，每年1000万美元，由州和地方政府资助，并向养羊和养牛的农民征收围栏税。围栏由一队巡逻员维护，他们分担长度，每人负责一段。道格指着一个被补上的洞。看到这块网了吗？

23
